# @@NAME@@

A [collage](https://github.com/Elagoht/collage) project, scaffolded by
`collage new`.

## Running

```
go mod tidy
go run .
```

```
go test ./...
```

Or `collage dev` from inside this directory. Either way the site is at
http://localhost:3000; set `PORT` (or pass `-port`) to move it.

Editing a template and refreshing is enough to see the change — collage reloads
templates from disk in development, even though they are embedded. Changing Go
code still needs a restart.

## What is here

- `main.go` — configuration, the static mount, and the `collage` CLI contract.
- `home.go` — the two pages, their data handlers, and the sign-up form's
  action.
- `home_test.go` — the tests, which need no server and no port: `app.Handler()`
  is an ordinary `http.Handler`, so `net/http/httptest` drives it directly.
  They go through the same `newApp` that `main` uses, so what they exercise is
  the site that actually runs.
- `templates/layouts/default.html` — the shell. `{{slot "content"}}` marks
  where a page's content goes.
- `templates/pages/home.html` — served at `/`. Declared `Static()`, so
  `collage export` renders it to a file.
- `templates/pages/signup.html` — served at `/signup`, with a form. Declared
  `Dynamic()`, because **a form needs a server to post to**: a static build
  skips this page and says so.
- `static/app.css` — served at `/static/`, and copied into a static build.

## Three things worth knowing early

**Templates and static files are embedded.** `//go:embed` in `main.go` means
this binary runs from any working directory — a container, a systemd unit, a
copy on a server. It costs nothing while developing: with development mode on,
collage prefers the directory on disk whenever it is there.

**The stylesheet is linked with `{{asset}}`.** That renders
`/static/app.<hash>.css`, served with a year-long `immutable` lifetime: a
changed file is a changed URL, so the browser never has to ask whether its copy
is still good. Writing `/static/app.css` by hand also works, but can only be
cached briefly.

**Set `COLLAGE_CSRF_KEY` before deploying.** It signs the tokens
`{{csrfToken}}` puts in forms. Without one, collage generates a key per process
and says so — fine while developing, wrong in production, because every form
submitted before a restart is refused after it and one instance refuses what
another issued.

```
COLLAGE_CSRF_KEY=$(head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64) go run .
```

## Deploying

There is no `collage start`: this is a Go program, so production is a binary.

```
collage build              # -> bin/@@NAME@@, built for linux/amd64
collage build -i           # also offers a Dockerfile and a systemd unit in bin/
HOST=0.0.0.0 PORT=8080 COLLAGE_CSRF_KEY=... ./bin/@@NAME@@
```

`.gitignore` ignores `bin/@@NAME@@`, the binary itself, rather than `bin/` — so a
`bin/Dockerfile` you edit is committed like any other file.

Templates and static files are embedded, so the binary is the whole site — nothing
to copy next to it, and a container image can be that binary and nothing else.
It traps `SIGTERM` and drains in-flight requests before exiting, answers `/healthz`
for a liveness check, and caches rendered pages under `.cache` so a restart does not
re-render everything.

It serves plain HTTP; put it behind whatever terminates TLS for you, or wrap
`app.Handler()` in an `http.Server` of your own. The full story is in the framework's
`docs/deployment.md`.

## The collage CLI contract

- **`collage dev`** runs `go run .` here with `COLLAGE_DEV=1` set. `main.go`
  reads that and turns on development mode.
- **`collage export`** runs `go run . -collage-build -out <dir>` (adding
  `-clean` when you used `collage export -clean`). `main.go` parses those flags
  and renders every statically-buildable page through `collage.NewBuilder` —
  collage's own builder, so this project gets its containment checks and its
  refusal to write into a dangerous `-out`, rather than a weaker copy.

If you rewrite `main.go`, keep both halves working, or `collage dev` and
`collage export` stop doing anything useful here.

## Next steps

- Add pages with `collage.NewPage` and fragments with `collage.NewFragment`.
- Give a page more fragments, each with its own data handler: sibling handlers
  run concurrently, so a page of independent parts fetches them at once.
- Open a fragment at its own URL with `WithFragmentPath`, and answer a form
  with `collage.RenderFragment` — that is how part of a page refreshes without
  a client framework.
- Add a `robots.txt`, a sitemap or a feed with `collage.NewDocument`: a routed,
  cached response whose handler returns bytes rather than rendering templates.
- Turn on caching with `Cache.Enabled`, and declare pages `Incremental(ttl)`.
- Read the documentation at https://github.com/Elagoht/collage.
